docs(autododge): research and implementation plan for an in-process C++ hook

Research and a phased plan for replacing the reactive auto-nexus with a hook inside
the .NET 9 client. No hook code is written and no game file is touched.

The findings, in short:

  * The client is pure IL with System.Runtime.TieredCompilation disabled, so every
    method is JIT-compiled exactly once and a detour is permanent. The JIT vtable is
    four slots with compileMethod at 0 and getVersionIdentifier at 2 -- verified by
    disassembly of the shipped clrjit.dll. Note that the community JIT-hook libraries
    hardcode slot 4, which was correct only up to .NET Core 3.1.

  * The client never applies damage to itself: Entity.HitBy* and
    Projectile.HitCharacter compute a damage number only to draw it, and the sole
    writer of Player.Health is the GmHealthUpdate handler. So the collision decision
    at Projectile.cs:1573 is genuinely ahead of the server's damage, which is the
    window this hook exists to occupy. Measured: the reflecting health reading
    arrives 55.5 ms median (98.1 ms p90) after that decision.

  * The hook does not need to reimplement the trajectories or the armour formula.
    ProjDesc.GetPosition and Entity.GetDamageTaken are both callable, which also
    makes prediction exactly consistent with the client's own collision test. The
    reference engine reached the same conclusion independently.

  * It does not make the nexus unnecessary in general, and that is measured rather
    than assumed: 74% of HP falls (571 of 775) have no client-side hit claim at all,
    so the server is the only witness for most damage.

Deliverables under autododgeresearch/: README index, CLIENT-FINDINGS, TIMING,
CLIENT-MAP, HOOK-ARCHITECTURE, DODGE-ALGORITHM, JAVA-BRIDGE, BRIDGE-RESEARCH,
IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN (six phases, spikes, exit criteria, tests, milestones) and
RISKS, plus the tools that produced every number.

Also: corrects an earlier reading of this project which claimed the client sends no
damage acknowledgement. It does -- GmHit (id 102), the local player as ObjectId in
161 of 580 captured packets -- but not generally enough to matter. And one verified
interaction bug is recorded: RequestEscape's cast branch cancels on movement
(World.cs:13043-13047), so a dodge that steers will cancel an in-progress nexus.
